const LLMS = "# WorthMyDegree\n\n> Free, anonymous college-ROI calculator. Enter a major, a school, and a\n> loan; see the 10-year financial outcome versus a debt-free high-school\n> graduate, under the 2026 US federal repayment rules. No login, no cost,\n> nothing sold. Built by a high-school student; every figure on screen\n> traces to a cited public source (the in-app Methodology section lists\n> every citation).\n\n## Pages\n\n- [Calculator](https://worthmydegree.com/): major + school + loan \u2192\n  10-year net position, break-even debt, monthly payment and take-home\n  pay by career stage, side-by-side comparison of two paths, the\n  community-college transfer path (2+2 or full associate's), and a\n  downloadable PDF report.\n- [Repayment plan comparison](https://worthmydegree.com/?tool=repayment):\n  for balances already owed \u2014 every current federal plan (Standard,\n  Extended, Tiered Standard, IBR, RAP) plus private loans, on the user's\n  actual balance and income, including forgiveness clocks and the\n  one-way-door asymmetry of switching into RAP.\n- [School search](https://worthmydegree.com/?tool=schools): field of\n  study + monthly budget + home state \u2192 every US college that fits the\n  budget, each priced at the rate that student would actually pay\n  (in-state vs out-of-state resolved per school).\n- [Graduate school search](https://worthmydegree.com/?tool=gradschools):\n  field of study + master's or doctorate + budget \u2192 graduate schools\n  priced from IPEDS published tuition, each shown beside what graduates\n  in that field actually borrowed. Tuition and fees only \u2014 no federal\n  source publishes graduate living costs.\n- [Infographics](https://worthmydegree.com/charts): single-picture\n  charts built from the same federal data as the calculator, free to\n  reuse anywhere. One page, each chart addressable by #slug fragment.\n\n## Data sources (all public, all cited in-app)\n\n- BLS OEWS wages, layered national \u2192 state \u2192 metro\n- BLS \"typical education needed for entry\" by occupation\n- NY Fed labor-market outcomes by major (unemployment, underemployment,\n  early/mid-career wages)\n- College Scorecard: cost of attendance and median debt for 5,000+\n  institutions; per-school medical/dental/law debt from the\n  field-of-study release\n- studentaid.gov published 2026 repayment rules \u2014 the RAP payment\n  function is verified row-by-row against the published AGI chart by an\n  automated check\n- CPS ASEC microdata for the age-earnings profile behind the\n  high-school-graduate baseline\n\n## Facts an agent should get right\n\n- Everything shown is an estimate for education, not financial advice.\n- Outcome data reflects who attends a school, not the school's causal\n  effect (selection vs. treatment); the tool discloses this.\n- The 2026 federal overhaul changed borrowing materially: Grad PLUS is\n  abolished, Parent PLUS is newly capped, and RAP is the default\n  income-driven plan for new loans. Advice describing SAVE/PAYE-era\n  rules is out of date.\n- Canonical host: worthmydegree.com. studentloanroi.com (redirects) and\n  studentloanroi.streamlit.app (legacy) are the same application.\n\n## Contact\n\nresearch@worthmydegree.com \u2014 student research project on whether outcome\ntransparency changes college decisions.\n\n## Guides\n\nPlain-English explainers on the 2026 federal loan rules and what a\ndegree costs, indexed at https://worthmydegree.com/guides. Each one\nends at the calculator.\n\n- [The repayment switch that only goes one way](https://worthmydegree.com/guides/switching-repayment-plans-2026):\n  Payments you have already made count toward RAP. RAP payments mostly\n  do not count back. On a graduate balance the door stays shut until\n  you earn about $108,000.\n- [What consolidating does to the plans you have left](https://worthmydegree.com/guides/consolidating-student-loans-2026):\n  A Direct Consolidation Loan made on or after July 1, 2026 leaves you\n  only RAP and the Tiered Standard Plan, for every federal loan you\n  hold. The older ones included.\n- [What starting at community college is actually worth](https://worthmydegree.com/guides/community-college-first):\n  Two years at a community college before transferring cuts the\n  published cost of a bachelor's by 39%, and changes how much of it\n  you can borrow in your own name.\n- [For parents: how to run your student's numbers in one sitting](https://worthmydegree.com/guides/for-parents-run-the-numbers):\n  A step-by-step walkthrough for parents. Turn a cost of attendance\n  into a monthly payment, a break-even number, and a PDF you can both\n  read.\n- [For counselors: the money conversation, in the time you actually have](https://worthmydegree.com/guides/for-counselors-the-money-conversation):\n  A free, no-login tool your students can use alone or in a meeting.\n  What changed under the 2026 federal rules, and the three\n  conversations it makes shorter.\n- [The Parent PLUS cap nobody mentions until senior year](https://worthmydegree.com/guides/parent-plus-senior-year):\n  The 2026 rules cap Parent PLUS at $20,000 a year \u2014 and $65,000 in\n  total. Those two numbers don't fit together, and the gap lands in\n  your final year.\n\n## Infographics\n\nFree single-picture charts built from the same federal data as the\ncalculator, indexed at https://worthmydegree.com/charts. Reusable\nanywhere with attribution. One page, so each is addressed by fragment.\n\n- [Borrow the federal maximum for a bachelor's and the plan barely matters](https://worthmydegree.com/charts#borrow-the-federal-maximum):\n  $31,000 is the most a dependent undergraduate can borrow federally.\n  On a $61,000 salary, income-driven, Standard and 2026 RAP all clear\n  it inside a decade, and the whole spread between them is $984.\n- [The 20 best-paid careers that need less than four years of school](https://worthmydegree.com/charts#careers-without-a-bachelors):\n  Total pay over the first ten years of work for the highest-paying\n  occupations BLS says need less than a bachelor's degree. All twenty\n  beat the median bachelor's-level career, and ten of them need no\n  college at all.\n- [Everyone says the rent eats the raise](https://worthmydegree.com/charts#rent-versus-the-raise):\n  San Francisco wages against the national median for the same job,\n  discounted by what it costs to live there. It is still ahead in all\n  nine occupations.\n- [Same degree, seven times the debt](https://worthmydegree.com/charts#same-degree-different-debt):\n  Median debt at graduation for the same professional degree runs from\n  $37,167 to $439,325 depending only on which school you attend.\n- [The family maximum is $92,000 and $65,000 of it is your parents'](https://worthmydegree.com/charts#the-family-maximum):\n  Everything the government will lend for four years of a dependent\n  undergraduate is $27,000 in the student's name and $65,000 in a\n  parent's. Parent PLUS sits outside every income-driven plan, so 71%\n  of the debt never moves with the repayment choice.\n- [The 20 careers that earn most in 30 years, and 18 are health care](https://worthmydegree.com/charts#top-earning-careers):\n  Everything earned in the 30 years after a bachelor's degree, capped\n  at each occupation's own published 90th percentile. Fourteen of the\n  twenty are physicians, and only two are reachable on a bachelor's\n  degree alone.\n- [You asked for $20,000. You pay back $31,070](https://worthmydegree.com/charts#what-the-loan-really-costs):\n  Every federal loan is billed for more than it hands over, because\n  the origination fee comes off at disbursement. On Parent PLUS you\n  sign for $20,883 to receive $20,000.\n- [What the data says is the best major](https://worthmydegree.com/charts#best-major-by-the-data):\n  The ten majors with the highest early-career pay, shown beside their\n  unemployment and underemployment rates.\n- [Business majors, ranked by what they actually pay](https://worthmydegree.com/charts#business-majors-tier-list):\n  Every business degree tiered by early-career pay and by the share of\n  graduates working in jobs that do not require the degree.\n- [College majors, ranked by the job market](https://worthmydegree.com/charts#majors-tier-list):\n  A tier list of college majors built from early-career pay,\n  unemployment and underemployment rather than reputation.\n- [The federal cap is doing the flattening](https://worthmydegree.com/charts#student-vs-parent-debt):\n  Median federal debt at graduation by field, students against\n  parents, showing how the Parent PLUS cap compresses what a family\n  can borrow.\n- [What a top university charges around the world](https://worthmydegree.com/charts#world-tuition):\n  Annual tuition for domestic undergraduates at each country's\n  highest-ranked university, converted to U.S. dollars.\n";
